{
  "id": "j",
  "candidate": "j-r1",
  "heard": "A global average can hide trouble across domains, too. A model can do well on one kind of writing and turn out to be much less sure somewhere else. Paloma keeps those slices visible. Give each domain its own column, and you can see where the probability assignments hold up and where they don't. A pooled score puts that detail back into one number. There is another problem when the only thing a leaderboard receives is a score. The bars are supposed to be probabilities, and all their mass together has to be exactly 1. A claimed score gives you no way to check that, so run the forward pass in a controlled sandbox, apply softmax to the logits, and check the total probability mass, then compute the loss yourself. Checking the sum is a useful guard, but the score still has to come from the actual model.",
